Since $y = mx + 4$ and $y = 3x + b$ intersect at $(6, 10)$, we know that $y$ equals both expressions at that point. Therefore, we can set the two equations equal to each other:
$$
\begin{align*}
mx + 4 &= 3x + b, \\
b &= 10 - 4 - 3(6), \quad \text{where} \ m = 3 \quad \Rightarrow \quad b = 10 - 4 - 18 \quad \Rightarrow \quad b = -12.
\end{align*}
$$
To find $m$, we substitute $b = -12$ into the equation $y = mx + 4$:
$$
\begin{align*}
y &= mx + 4 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 10 &= m(6) + 4 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 10 &= 6m + 4 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 6m = 6 \quad \Rightarrow \quad m = 1.
\end{align*}
$$
Now, we can find $b + m$:
$$
\begin{align*}
b + m &= -12 + 1 \quad \Rightarrow \quad b + m = -11.
\end{align*}
$$
Therefore, $\boxed{b + m = -11}$ is the sum.

