We want to find $f(x)$ for all non-zero $x$ and then set it to $f(-x)$ and see what we get.  We are told $f(x) + 2f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) = 3x.$  Let's set $x = 1/x.$  This gives $f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) + 2f \left( x \right) = \frac{3}{x}.$  We already know what $f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right)$ is in terms of $f \left( x \right),$ so we get\begin{align*}
2f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) & = \frac{3}{x} - f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) \\
3f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) & = \frac{3}{x} \\
f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) & = \frac{1}{x}.
\end{align*}Furthermore, we can use our original equation to get \begin{align*}
f \left( x \right) + 2f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) & = 3x \\
f \left( x \right) + 2 \cdot \frac{1}{x} & = 3x \\
f \left( x \right) & = 3x - \frac{2}{x}.
\end{align*}Plugging $\frac{1}{x}$ into $3x - \frac{2}{x},$ we get \begin{align*}
f \left( \frac{1}{x} \right) & = 3 \cdot \frac{1}{x} - \frac{2}{\frac{1}{x}} \\
& = 3x - 2x \\
& = x.
\end{align*}Therefore, $\boxed{0, \pm \frac{2 \sqrt{3}}{3}}$ are our real solutions. The answer is 0,\pm\frac{2\sqrt{3}}{3}

